1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,820
These robots were trained to jump
into molten steel on purpose,

2
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:06,238
by their own company.

3
00:00:06,318 --> 00:00:09,518
Figure, the robot maker,
says the jumps were real,

4
00:00:09,558 --> 00:00:13,518
and a spokesperson for the Finnish
steel plant says she watched them go in.

5
00:00:13,718 --> 00:00:15,678
So why would a company do this?

6
00:00:15,758 --> 00:00:19,038
And how does a robot learn a
jump it only gets to make once?

7
00:00:19,488 --> 00:00:20,808
Start with why.

8
00:00:21,088 --> 00:00:25,128
These are F.02s, Figure's
second-generation humanoid.

9
00:00:25,408 --> 00:00:29,008
Figure says they spent months
loading parts at a BMW plant.

10
00:00:29,088 --> 00:00:33,488
But as its newer F.03 fleet grew, keeping
the old ones running stopped making

11
00:00:33,568 --> 00:00:36,928
sense. So what do you
do with a retired robot?

12
00:00:37,048 --> 00:00:40,426
Sell it? Figure says they're packed
with its own custom hardware,

13
00:00:40,506 --> 00:00:43,786
like the actuators that move the joints,
and it didn't want that leaving the

14
00:00:43,986 --> 00:00:45,754
building. Take them apart?

15
00:00:45,914 --> 00:00:49,754
Figure says doing that for every robot
would eat so much engineering time,

16
00:00:49,794 --> 00:00:51,594
it would delay the next model.

17
00:00:51,674 --> 00:00:53,434
Melt them? Simple.

18
00:00:53,634 --> 00:00:57,434
Except a robot that jumps in on
its own brings its battery along.

19
00:00:57,594 --> 00:00:59,034
Figure says foundries in the U.S.

20
00:00:59,114 --> 00:01:00,794
and Mexico refused.

21
00:01:00,874 --> 00:01:03,314
One in Imatra, Finland, said yes.

22
00:01:03,594 --> 00:01:05,714
Recycling old hardware is normal.

23
00:01:05,914 --> 00:01:07,754
Bringing a film crew is not.

24
00:01:08,154 --> 00:01:09,862
Blame a reply on X.

25
00:01:10,182 --> 00:01:15,302
In August, Figure's CEO, Brett Adcock,
asked what to do with the old robots.

26
00:01:15,542 --> 00:01:18,610
Arnold Schwarzenegger replied,
"You should melt them."

27
00:01:18,690 --> 00:01:23,330
In Terminator 2, his robot character
ends up lowered into molten steel so no

28
00:01:23,410 --> 00:01:27,498
one can copy its technology, which
is almost exactly Figure's reason.

29
00:01:27,538 --> 00:01:30,618
Hollywood wrote the business case in 1991.

30
00:01:30,698 --> 00:01:33,978
Figure took the hint, and Schwarzenegger
appears in the finished film.

31
00:01:34,378 --> 00:01:35,818
Now the hard part.

32
00:01:36,058 --> 00:01:38,858
You can't rehearse a jump
like this in a real furnace.

33
00:01:39,098 --> 00:01:42,698
So Figure says it trained a
new AI model in simulation.

34
00:01:42,938 --> 00:01:45,338
Here's how that kind of
training generally works.

35
00:01:45,578 --> 00:01:49,098
A virtual robot tries the jump,
and the attempt gets a score.

36
00:01:49,338 --> 00:01:51,618
Did it stay balanced? Push off cleanly?

37
00:01:51,818 --> 00:01:56,286
Land on target? The software nudges the
controls toward whatever scored well,

38
00:01:56,326 --> 00:02:00,966
and it tries again, over and over,
in many virtual copies at once.

39
00:02:01,246 --> 00:02:04,126
Virtual robots can fall
on their faces all day,

40
00:02:04,246 --> 00:02:06,526
and nobody files an incident report.

41
00:02:06,686 --> 00:02:09,726
Figure says it used stunt
performers' movements as a reference,

42
00:02:09,806 --> 00:02:11,806
a style guide for a good leap.

43
00:02:11,846 --> 00:02:16,366
The result is called a policy: a learned
rule that turns what the robot senses

44
00:02:16,486 --> 00:02:19,134
into motor commands, many times a second.

45
00:02:19,214 --> 00:02:22,041
If a foot lands a little
early, the sensors catch it,

46
00:02:22,067 --> 00:02:25,854
and the policy adjusts the knee
and ankle before the robot tips.

47
00:02:25,934 --> 00:02:27,774
Not a wish, not a plan.

48
00:02:27,894 --> 00:02:30,254
More like a very well-rehearsed reflex.

49
00:02:30,654 --> 00:02:33,374
But a simulator is never
quite the real world.

50
00:02:33,774 --> 00:02:36,974
Real joints have slack,
floors aren't perfectly flat,

51
00:02:37,054 --> 00:02:41,214
and sensors are noisy. And Figure says
this jump had to work at a foundry the

52
00:02:41,294 --> 00:02:42,574
team had never visited.

53
00:02:43,054 --> 00:02:46,294
A common fix is to make the
simulation messy on purpose.

54
00:02:46,494 --> 00:02:50,334
Change the weight, the footing,
the timing, a little every round,

55
00:02:50,414 --> 00:02:53,334
so the policy can't depend
on one perfect world.

56
00:02:53,614 --> 00:02:55,614
We don't know which
variations Figure used.

57
00:02:56,014 --> 00:03:00,334
It also rehearsed for real, with robots
jumping from a second floor onto giant

58
00:03:00,414 --> 00:03:03,454
airbags. And the foundry did get messy.

59
00:03:03,534 --> 00:03:07,214
Figure says the heat and electromagnetic
noise knocked out cameras and other

60
00:03:07,294 --> 00:03:10,094
electronics, and the
policies kept running anyway.

61
00:03:10,494 --> 00:03:12,414
So what does it prove?

62
00:03:12,454 --> 00:03:15,614
That a trained jump can
survive a brutal environment.

63
00:03:15,654 --> 00:03:18,414
But it was one movement,
for one kind of target,

64
00:03:18,454 --> 00:03:20,974
in a setting people
prepared and supervised.

65
00:03:21,054 --> 00:03:24,494
That's not a robot that can walk
into any factory and work things out.

66
00:03:24,944 --> 00:03:27,344
So why is it uncomfortable to watch?

67
00:03:27,584 --> 00:03:28,944
Partly, it's the shape.

68
00:03:29,344 --> 00:03:33,264
When something with arms, legs and
a head walks to the edge and leaps,

69
00:03:33,344 --> 00:03:35,024
it looks like someone making a choice.

70
00:03:35,424 --> 00:03:37,184
But the choice wasn't the robot's.

71
00:03:37,584 --> 00:03:40,864
People picked the ending, built
the target, and wrote the score.

72
00:03:41,104 --> 00:03:43,264
The policy just got very
good at reaching it.

73
00:03:43,744 --> 00:03:47,184
Does retiring hardware feel different
when the hardware looks like us?

74
00:03:47,424 --> 00:03:49,064
These robots didn't want anything.

75
00:03:49,184 --> 00:03:50,544
They just stuck the landing.

